WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:04.480
You can't play a child-murdering clown and
expect to walk away unscathed.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:08.900
When you get into the mindset of an evil ancient
entity, you might wind up in some pretty dark

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.780
places, like Bill Skarsgard has.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:14.380
Here's why the Swedish star has never quite
been the same since It.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:19.860
Pennywise is, without a doubt, Skarsgard's
most famous role to date, and for a good reason.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:23.880
The actor's portrayal of a killer clown has
received near-universal praise among critics

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:24.880
and filmgoers.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:28.240
One of the key reasons for all of the praise
is the physicality of the role.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.820
Regarding his part, the actor told the New
York Times,

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:33.520
"Everything I did took 100 percent of my energy.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:37.760
It was by far the most exhausting character
I've ever done, physically and mentally."

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:41.940
In the Blu-Ray special feature "Pennywise
Lives!," Skarsgard spoke about the physical

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:46.140
considerations of playing Pennywise, saying
he often passed on using stunt doubles.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:52.380
"Pennywise's physicality must be Pennywise's
physicality, and I wanted to bring my movement

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.320
and physicality in as much as I could."

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:59.059
Except for physically dangerous scenes, Skarsgard
insisted on always playing Pennywise himself,

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:02.580
a decision that frequently left him utterly
exhausted on set.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.320
How does one begin to portray an inhuman shapeshifter
that literally feeds off of the fear of other

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:09.040
beings?

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:12.960
One way that Skarsgard went about this was
to think about how Pennywise interacted with

00:01:12.969 --> 00:01:14.670
the other characters in the film.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:16.569
Skarsgard told Entertainment Weekly,

00:01:16.569 --> 00:01:19.979
"I liken every character that I do to a relationship
that you're in.

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:24.450
Pennywise and Bill go into this sort of relationship
together, and I'm trying to figure out who

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:27.999
he is, and I have to devote so much time and
effort to this other person - or thing, in

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.360
this case - and that goes on for months."

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:35.200
In an interview with Collider, Skarsgard further
elaborated on his approach to playing Pennywise,

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.700
telling them about how he tried to get into
Pennywise's head to better understand the

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:39.560
creature, saying,

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.720
"It really enjoyed being the clown.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:43.480
He preferred to take the shape of the clown.

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:45.279
Obviously, that opened ways of thinking.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:47.000
Why does he prefer being the clown?

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.120
But also there is this sense of enjoyment.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:52.320
The entity that is 'It' is enjoying being
the clown.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.700
There were a lot of abstract ways to look
at it.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:58.340
I tried to take all of those into consideration
when I embodied the character."

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:03.700
While Skarsgard managed to scare audiences
around the world, his fellow actors also found

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:07.300
themselves frequently unsettled by Pennywise
while working on the film.

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:11.459
Because the majority of It surrounds Pennywise
with the members of the so-called Losers'

00:02:11.459 --> 00:02:15.680
Club, many of the scenes require Skarsgard
to work directly with the child actors.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:20.250
To keep the children's reactions as realistic
as possible, Skarsgard tried to stay in character

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:21.650
whenever he was working.

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:23.260
He explained to Interview magazine,

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:25.920
"On set, I wasn't very friendly or goofy.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:29.420
I tried to maintain some sort of weirdness
about the character, at least when I was in

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:30.420
all the makeup."

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:34.940
Additionally, when he was in costume, Skarsgard
was kept completely separate from the actors

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:37.160
playing his young victims between takes.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:41.500
Because of this, the children had no idea
what Pennywise would look like in each scene.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:45.460
Bill recalled when he realized just how effective
this separation tactic was.

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:46.480
He told Interview,

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:48.160
"This one kid started crying.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:50.460
He started to cry and the director yelled,
'Action!'

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:53.320
And when they say 'action,' I am completely
in character.

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:56.879
So some of these kids got terrified and started
to cry in the middle of the take, and then

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:58.180
I realized, 'Holy s---.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:02:59.260
What am I doing?

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:00.000
What is this?

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.000
This is horrible.'"

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:06.540
When preparing to play in It, Bill Skarsgard
saw his connection to Pennywise as a toxic

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:09.100
relationship, although one that had some good
moments.

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.000
As he explained to Entertainment Weekly,

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:13.620
"It's just like being in a very destructive
relationship.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:17.540
[…] All your friends go, 'You need to dump
this piece of s---, he or she is destroying

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:18.360
your life.'

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:21.480
And then once you're out of it, you see, 'I
was so miserable.'

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:25.880
But I wouldn't say I was miserable doing Pennywise
because I had a lot of fun with it, as well."

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:30.140
Considering the emotional toll of playing
Pennywise, it's no surprise that Skarsgard

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:32.480
felt relief after filming for It concluded.

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:36.400
"Every scene I'm in in this film is just 100%
energy at all times."

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:40.640
He told EW that after finishing up the first
movie, he went back to his childhood home

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:41.400
in Sweden.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:45.280
And one day, he was visiting with his mom,
enjoying the coffee and the company, when

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:47.880
he suddenly realized that he was free from
Pennywise.

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:48.820
He explained,

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:52.800
"It was a very quick shift of just feeling
better, like, 'Oh my god, I'm relieved that

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:55.140
I don't have to deal with the darkness of
the character.

00:03:55.140 --> 00:04:00.180
I likened it to an exorcism - him exiting
my body and getting rid of the Pennywise toxins."

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:03.820
Skarsgard's Pennywise didn't just scare moviegoers.

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:07.720
Skarsgard himself was haunted by the killer
clown even after filming concluded.

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:09.340
He told Entertainment Weekly,

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:14.260
"I was home, done with the movie, and I started
having very strange and vivid Pennywise dreams.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:16.200
Every night, he came and visited."

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:20.100
Skarsgard further elaborated on the dreams,
saying that they generally took one of two

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:20.980
forms.

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:24.280
In one, he was having to deal with Pennywise
as "a separate entity."

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:28.940
In the other type, he found himself as Pennywise
in weird situations, like "out in public,"

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:30.200
with people staring at him.

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:35.000
Though the thought of Pennywise haunting someone's
dreams is without a doubt a terrifying premise,

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:38.720
the actor sees his nightly visits from Pennywise
in a more optimistic light.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:43.720
Skarsgard believes these dreams are his psyche
attempting to, quote, "let the character go."

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:47.180
Despite these dark dreams, Skarsgard thinks
he's in a good place.

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:48.680
As he explained to EW,

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:49.800
"It was amazing.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:52.920
It's a daunting but exciting thing to sort
of revisit him again.

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:53.920
[…] I'm good with it."

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:58.940
Despite the trouble Bill Skarsgard went through
after playing Pennywise the first time around,

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:02.560
it was inevitable that he'd have to step back
into his clown shoes for the sequel.

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:07.800
After spending so much time and devoting so
much emotional energy to being free of Pennywise,

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:10.961
Skarsgard told Collider in an interview that
he didn't have any trouble getting back in

00:05:10.961 --> 00:05:12.400
touch with the dancing clown.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:13.520
As he explained,

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:16.380
"I was surprised how much of the character
was just there already.

00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:20.290
I instantly could access him again, like it
was yesterday that we wrapped the first one.

00:05:20.290 --> 00:05:21.840
It was just all there.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:25.690
So, the work and preparation and figuring
out the character is almost intuitive, which

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:26.740
is pretty cool."

